        *   The comment "This approach uses DOM manipulation which might not be suitable in all Node.js environments" is accurate. If the code is intended to run *only* in Node.js without a DOM simulation (like JSDOM), the `document` part will fail, and the fallback will be used. If it's intended to run in a browser, the fallback will never be used. The problem statement doesn't specify the execution environment beyond the Node.js entrypoint.
